From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: paulneja <207793167+paulneja@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 66/66] esp32s3: the USB port becomes a host

The two halves of the S3's single USB PHY cannot drive it at the same time,
so giving the port to the OTG controller takes the Serial/JTAG tty away: the
acm node goes off, and console=ttyGS3 goes out of the kernel command line
with it. What is left is UART0 on D0/D1, which this image already carries an
earlycon and a getty for. dr_mode is host because there is no gadget stack
here to be a device with, which is also what makes USB_DWC2_HOST -- the
default that Kconfig choice takes when USB_GADGET is off -- the only mode
built.

dr_mode, the phy's status and the acm node's status are one decision written
in three places, so they are one patch.

The dwc2 params setter is the part worth arguing about.
dwc2_set_default_params() takes host_dma from the core's own capability, and
on this core that capability is probably real. But the RAM Linux was given is
the 8 MB of octal PSRAM at 0x3d800000, and the USB master's window is
understood to cover internal SRAM only. A host_dma the core cannot actually
reach does not fail loudly: the transfers go somewhere, and the symptom is
corruption rather than an error, on a board where a checksum mismatch is
easier to blame on the USB device than on the bus. PIO costs nothing here --
the port is full speed and the CPU has 12 Mbit/s to spare -- so the setter
forces it, and the comment records that this is the one line to revisit if
the master's window turns out to include PSRAM.

Two things to watch on the board, both named because a log will not show
them:

  - The FIFO sizes in the device tree (g-rx-fifo-size = <64> and the rest)
    were written before anything used the port as a host, and dwc2 warns
    rather than failing when they are short. If enumeration works and
    transfers stall or truncate, that is the first thing to raise.
  - This image drives no VBUS regulator -- nothing in the device tree
    describes one and dwc2 has no vbus-supply to reach for -- so the port
    powers nothing. The peripheral needs its own supply, which is what the
    hub's USB-C input is for. A device that enumerates and then resets
    itself is usually a power problem, not a driver.

---
 arch/xtensa/boot/dts/arduino-nano-esp32.dts | 18 +++++++++++++++++-
 arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3.dtsi           |  2 +-
 drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c                      | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 drivers/usb/dwc2/params.c                   | 84 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 119 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/arduino-nano-esp32.dts b/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/arduino-nano-esp32.dts
index aaf3d758eb3c7..901246be297f1 100644
--- a/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/arduino-nano-esp32.dts
+++ b/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/arduino-nano-esp32.dts
@@ -135,10 +135,26 @@ panel@0 {
 	};
 };
 
-&acm {
+/* The S3 has one USB PHY and two peripherals that can drive it, so the
+ * two halves are mutually exclusive. Handing it to the OTG core here is
+ * what costs this image the Serial/JTAG tty: ttyGS3 exists only while
+ * the acm node is enabled, and the console is UART0 on D0/D1 instead.
+ * The switch is a register write, not an eFuse, so putting the board
+ * into download mode by hand still brings the port back to the ROM. */
+&usb_phy {
+	status = "ok";
+};
+
+&usb {
+	/* Host-only: this image has no gadget stack to be a device with. */
+	dr_mode = "host";
 	status = "ok";
 };
 
+&acm {
+	status = "disabled";
+};
+
 &ipc {
 	wifi@1 {
 		compatible = "esp,esp32-wifi-shmem";
diff --git a/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3.dtsi b/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3.dtsi
index 7e58a18c5d9a2..a1f9639aa7ec1 100644
--- a/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3.dtsi
+++ b/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3.dtsi
@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@ / {
 	interrupt-parent = <&pic>;
 
 	chosen {
-		bootargs = "earlycon=esp32s3uart,mmio32,0x60000000,115200n8,40000000 console=ttyGS3 console=ttyS0,115200n8 rw root=mtd:rootfs rootfstype=cramfs panic=10 panic_console_replay ";
+		bootargs = "earlycon=esp32s3uart,mmio32,0x60000000,115200n8,40000000 console=ttyS0,115200n8 rw root=mtd:rootfs rootfstype=cramfs panic=10 panic_console_replay ";
 	};
 
 	aliases {
diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc2/params.c b/drivers/usb/dwc2/params.c
index 091bfcfef7530..f3a0e3de5ffa9 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/params.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/params.c
@@ -303,6 +303,89 @@ static void dwc2_set_stm32mp15_hsotg_params(struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg)
 	p->hird_threshold_en = false;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The ESP32-S3's USB DMA does not reach the RAM this kernel runs from. Linux
+ * is given the 8 MB of octal PSRAM at 0x3d800000, outside the internal SRAM
+ * window the USB core's master can address, so a buffer the host driver hands
+ * to the core would be read from the wrong memory -- and the core reports no
+ * error for that, it returns whatever the bus gave it. Held to PIO, the same
+ * transfers run through the CPU and the FIFO, which at 12 Mbit/s full speed is
+ * far below what this core can copy.
+ *
+ * dwc2_set_default_params() takes host_dma from the core's own capability and
+ * runs before this, so the value set here is what stands. If the USB master's
+ * window turns out to include PSRAM, this is the one line to revisit.
+ *
+ * The FIFO sizes are the other half, and they are not a tuning choice: the
+ * core reports 200 words of FIFO in GHWCFG3.DFIFO_DEPTH, and
+ * dwc2_set_default_params() takes the host sizes from the registers without
+ * checking that they fit. dwc2_calculate_dynamic_fifo() then finds the sum
+ * over budget and falls back to Method 2, which wants 516 + host_channels +
+ * 256 + 768 = 1548 words -- more than the part has. (The 1024 in that formula
+ * is the spec's assumed maximum packet size for a periodic endpoint, not this
+ * core's FIFO depth.) It cannot allocate that either, so it prints
+ * "invalid fifo sizes" and leaves whatever the registers held. The receive
+ * FIFO is then too small to hold a control transfer's response, which is why
+ * a device is seen to attach and then times out on every descriptor read:
+ * the port detects the pull-up, issues SETUP, and loses the reply.
+ *
+ * The split below is ESP-IDF's, from its own host driver for this same core.
+ * components/usb/hcd_dwc.c carries three tables -- default, biased to RX and
+ * biased to periodic TX -- and the one taken here is the RX-biased one:
+ *
+ *	default		rx 104	nptx 48	ptx  48
+ *	bias_rx		rx 152	nptx 16	ptx  32		<- this one
+ *	bias_ptx	rx  34	nptx 16	ptx 150
+ *
+ * The vendor's own comment for it is the sizing rule: the RX FIFO wants
+ * ((LPS / 4) * 2) + 2 lines to hold two maximum-size packets at the largest
+ * packet size, the non-periodic TX FIFO wants (LPS / 4) * n for n packets of
+ * the largest size, and the periodic FIFO takes what is left. At full speed
+ * LPS is 64 bytes, so 16 words is one packet and 152 is nine -- far more than
+ * the two the rule asks for, which is the point of the RX bias: a host that
+ * reads descriptors and does bulk IN is almost always waiting on the receive
+ * side.
+ *
+ * Note what the vendor does not do: it does not spend the core's whole FIFO.
+ * usb_dwc_hal.h defines USB_DWC_HAL_FIFO_TOTAL_USABLE_LINES as 200 with the
+ * comment that although the core has 256 lines only 200 are usable, because
+ * EPINFO_CTL reserves the rest. Every one of the three tables sums to exactly
+ * 200. So the numbers below are deliberately small, and a larger split is not
+ * a free improvement -- it is a departure from the only sizing for this
+ * silicon that has been tested.
+ *
+ * dwc2 checks each of these against the corresponding register's own value
+ * and resets it to that value with a warning if it is larger, so the values
+ * printed below are the ones that stand. The three sum to 200.
+ */
+static void dwc2_set_esp32s3_params(struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg)
+{
+	struct dwc2_core_params *p = &hsotg->params;
+
+	p->host_dma = false;
+
+	p->host_rx_fifo_size = 152;
+	p->host_nperio_tx_fifo_size = 16;
+	p->host_perio_tx_fifo_size = 32;
+
+	/*
+	 * The split that stands, and the core's own total for comparison.
+	 * These are params, not hw_params: hw_params is filled in by
+	 * dwc2_get_hwparams() before dwc2_init_params() calls this, so it
+	 * holds the core's ceiling (256/256/256 here) and never the numbers
+	 * just written. Reading it back is useless for the same reason --
+	 * the FIFO registers are not programmed until dwc2_config_fifos()
+	 * runs later in host init, which is where the read-back lives.
+	 */
+	dev_info(hsotg->dev,
+		 "fifo: total=%u host_channels=%u rx=%u nptx=%u ptx=%u\n",
+		 hsotg->hw_params.total_fifo_size,
+		 hsotg->hw_params.host_channels,
+		 p->host_rx_fifo_size,
+		 p->host_nperio_tx_fifo_size,
+		 p->host_perio_tx_fifo_size);
+}
+
 const struct of_device_id dwc2_of_match_table[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "brcm,bcm2835-usb", .data = dwc2_set_bcm_params },
 	{ .compatible = "hisilicon,hi6220-usb", .data = dwc2_set_his_params },
@@ -319,6 +402,7 @@ const struct of_device_id dwc2_of_match_table[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "lantiq,arx100-usb", .data = &dwc2_set_ltq_ase_params },
 	{ .compatible = "lantiq,xrx200-usb", .data = &dwc2_set_ltq_xrx200_params },
 	{ .compatible = "lantiq,xrx300-usb", .data = &dwc2_set_ltq_xrx200_params },
+	{ .compatible = "esp,esp32s3-usb", .data = dwc2_set_esp32s3_params },
 	{ .compatible = "snps,dwc2" },
 	{ .compatible = "samsung,s3c6400-hsotg",
 	  .data = dwc2_set_s3c6400_params },
diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c b/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c
index 2414291aa9087..d67d7c4b5b325 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c
@@ -295,6 +295,23 @@ static void dwc2_config_fifos(struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg)
 	dev_dbg(hsotg->dev, "new hptxfsiz=%08x\n",
 		dwc2_readl(hsotg, HPTXFSIZ));
 
+	/*
+	 * Read the three registers back, because everything above this line
+	 * is a claim about what was written and this is the only place that
+	 * can show it landed. dwc2_calculate_dynamic_fifo() has already had
+	 * its say by now -- if the split did not fit it printed "invalid
+	 * fifo sizes" and left the registers alone, and the numbers here
+	 * then disagree with the ones dwc2_set_esp32s3_params() logged.
+	 * The start addresses are worth printing too: they are derived from
+	 * the depths, so a wrong start with a right depth means the layout
+	 * is wrong even though each size looks plausible on its own.
+	 */
+	dev_info(hsotg->dev,
+		 "fifo: grxfsiz=%08x gnptxfsiz=%08x hptxfsiz=%08x\n",
+		 dwc2_readl(hsotg, GRXFSIZ),
+		 dwc2_readl(hsotg, GNPTXFSIZ),
+		 dwc2_readl(hsotg, HPTXFSIZ));
+
 	if (hsotg->params.en_multiple_tx_fifo &&
 	    hsotg->hw_params.snpsid >= DWC2_CORE_REV_2_91a) {
 		/*
